<section xml:id="graphs_search-example"
         xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">

  <title>Search Example : Friend Finder</title>

  <p> As a concrete example of using graph search, we will consider the problem of determining a way to get a message from one person to another. We will rely on the same friendship graph structure we saw earlier:</p>

  <figure>
    <caption>Friendship connections between individuals.</caption>
    <image source="images/graphs/graphs-intro-2"
           width="20%">
        <shortdescription>Nodes for Anna, Ben, Charlie, Diego, Erin and Fred.</shortdescription>
        <description><p>
           Nodes for Anna, Ben, Charlie, Diego, Erin and Fred. Erin is connected to Charlie,
            Diego and Fred. Diego is also connected to Anna and Ben. Anna and Ben are connected. 
        </p></description>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <p>We want to figure out how Anna can get a message to Fred. Finding the shortest path would make sense, as it would minimize the number of intermediaries needed to deliver the message. So we will use breadth-first search.</p>

  <p>
    First, we need to set up the graph. The <pf>addEdge</pf> function will add an edge between two vertices, and if the vertices do not already exist, it will add them to the graph as well. We do not want edge weights, but we do want the edges to be undirected, so we will set the weight to <pf>1</pf> and the directed flag to <pf>false</pf>. That will cause adding an edge from Anna to Ben to also add an edge from Ben to Anna.
  </p>

  <program>
    <![CDATA[
  Graph<string> graph;

  // edge between Anna and Ben with a weight of 1, undirected
  graph.addEdge("Anna", "Ben", 1, false);
  // add the rest of the edges in the graph
  graph.addEdge("Anna", "Diego", 1, false);
  ...
    ]]>
  </program>

  <p> We then need to implement the BFS algorithm. Translating our <xref ref="graphs_bfs-bfs-pseudocode" text="custom">BFS pseudocode</xref> into actual code looks like this:</p>

  <program>
<xi:include href="../../programs/graphs/search-example-bfs.cpp" parse="text"/>
  </program>

  <p>Finally, we can perform the search and print the path from Anna to Fred:</p>

  <listing xml:id="graphs_search-example-listing">
    <title></title>
    <program interactive="activecode"
             label="graphs_search-example"
             compile-also="graph-code_graph-cxx" 
             highlight-lines="29-37">
<xi:include href="../../programs/graphs/search-example.cpp" parse="text"/>
    </program>
  </listing>

  <exercise label="graphs_search-example-ex-1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        What is the path produced by <xref ref="graphs_search-example-listing"/>? (Exactly as it is output.)
      </p>
      <p>
        <fillin width="40" mode="string" answer="Fred &lt;- Erin &lt;- Diego &lt;- Anna"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <strcmp>Fred &lt;- Erin &lt;- Diego &lt;- Anna</strcmp>
        </test>
        <test>
          <strcmp>.*</strcmp>
          <feedback>
            <p>Remember that our edges need to point from the course to its prerequisite.</p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
  </exercise>

</section>